AB  - The authors regret the need to provide further clarification regarding certain details of this study. Additionally, we apologize for not properly acknowledging the work of Wu et al. (2024), which largely inspired and, in essence, contributed to our research. (1) To summarize the state of the art on which the study is based, specific statements in the third paragraph of the Introduction need to be corrected. “However, few studies have attempted to construct an ML modeling framework between iWQP and dissolved-phase NAPL plumes to predict the future distribution of contamination plumes.” should be corrected to “However, due to the limited availability of suitable field datasets, few studies have attempted to construct an ML modeling framework between iWQP and dissolved-phase NAPL plumes to predict the future distribution of contamination plumes. Recently, Wu et al. (2024) simulated the fate of hydrocarbons in groundwater under various realistic conditions using a reactive transport model as a novel approach to explore when, where, and why correlations occur between iWQP and the components of dissolved petroleum hydrocarbons. These findings established a crucial foundation for the subsequent development of alternative predictive models utilizing machine learning techniques.”(2) To better emphasize the contribution of Wu et al.'s publicly available codes to this study, additional direct reference to the DOI of their code sets is necessary. Accordingly, in Section 2.2, “We acquired the necessary datasets for this study, including dissolved-phase NAPL data and iWQP data, by making minor adjustments to the code set developed by Wu et al. (2024).” should be corrected to “We acquired the reactive transport model (files) from Wu et al. (2024, 2025), and used them in this study with some adjustments to obtain dissolved-phase NAPL data and iWQP data.” We sincerely thank C. L. R. Wu, Dr R. M. Wagterveld and Dr B. M. van Breukelen for publicly making their reaction transport model code available.(3) “In-situ groundwater quality parameters (iWQPs)” and “secondary water quality impacts (SWQIs)” are not commonly used terms in the literature. Wu et al. (2024) were the first to introduce the term “iWQP” in the context of organic contamination hydrogeology, while Ng et al. (2014; 2015) coined the term “SWQI.” However, an error was made in the Introduction section when citing these terms. As a correction, in the third paragraph of the Introduction, “The transport and degradation of dissolved-phase NAPL triggers complex biogeochemical reactions that lead to secondary water quality impacts (SWQI) in contaminated aquifers, causing alterations in-situ groundwater quality parameters (iWQP), including pH, dissolved oxygen (DO), oxidation–reduction potential (ORP), and electrical conductivity (EC) (Ng et al., 2015).” should be corrected to “The transport and degradation of dissolved-phase NAPL triggers complex biogeochemical reactions that lead to secondary water quality impacts (SWQI) (Ng et al., 2014; Ng et al., 2015) in contaminated aquifers, causing alterations in-situ groundwater quality parameters (iWQP) (Wu et al., 2024), including pH, dissolved oxygen (DO), oxidation–reduction potential (ORP), and electrical conductivity (EC).”The authors would like to apologize for any inconvenience caused. CRediT authorship contribution statement Fei Qiao: Writing – original draft, Visualization, Methodology, Data curation. Jinguo Wang: Supervision, Funding acquisition, Conceptualization. Jian Song: Writing – review & editing. Zhou Chen: Supervision. Albert Kwame Kwaw: Writing – review & editing. Yongsheng Zhao: Investigation. Shiyu Zheng: Conceptualization. © 2025
