AB  - In-situ saccharification coupled with dark fermentation (ISS–DF) has emerged as a promising route for lignocellulosic biomass valorization to biohydrogen. However, improving biochemical conversion efficiency remains a major challenge. Here, this study propose a coordinated multi-metal strategy optimized by a Liquid Neural Network–Bayesian Optimization (LNN–BO) framework to enhance ISS and DF performance. The LNN–BO model achieved an R² of 0.90682, and the optimal ion concentrations were identified as 73.0 mg/L Fe²⁺, 77.6 mg/L Mg²⁺, and 42.7 mg/L Cu²⁺. Under these conditions, the reducing-sugar yield increased by 21.9% and cumulative hydrogen production increased by 37.5% to 113.1 ± 11.8 mL/g TS relative to the control. Model interpretation indicated that Cu²⁺ exerted a dominant inhibitory effect at higher levels, Mg²⁺ displayed structure- and dose-dependent regulation, and Fe²⁺ acted synergistically with both ions. The optimized system improved ISS–DF stability by enhancing metal utilization and strengthening pH buffering and redox performance. Thermodynamic analysis further suggested that co-supplementation promoted activation of a more exergonic butyrate-associated route, thereby improving overall energy-conversion efficiency. Microbial analyses revealed a dynamic hydrogen-producing consortium dominated by Clostridiaceae , Enterobacteriaceae , and Lachnospiraceae , consistent with enhanced functional complementarity and resilience. Metagenomic results supported these observations by showing upregulation of glycolysis-related genes ( PFK , GAPDH ) and butyrate-formation genes ( PTB , buk ). Finally, a techno-economic assessment for a 5 t/day wheat-straw facility yielded an investment return rate of 19.75% with a payback period of 6.1 years, highlighting the scalability of this strategy for lignocellulosic biohydrogen production. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
